title: A flip-flop that flips
mode: vhdl
chapter: Memory
check: behaves
cycles: 100
---- intro
Feed a flip-flop its own value, turned round, and it flips at every edge: 0, 1, 0, 1.

An output cannot be read inside the architecture, only written. So the value is kept in a **signal** `t`, which can be both, and the output is connected to it:

```
t <= not t;     -- inside the clock edge
q <= t;         -- outside the process
```
---- task
Make `q` flip at every rising edge of `clk`, keeping the value in the signal `t`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity toggle is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic
  );
end toggle;

architecture rtl of toggle is
    signal t : std_logic := '0';
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity toggle is
  port(
    clk : in  std_logic;
    q   : out std_logic
  );
end toggle;

architecture rtl of toggle is
    signal t : std_logic := '0';
begin
    process (clk)
    begin
        if rising_edge(clk) then
            t <= not t;
        end if;
    end process;
    q <= t;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Two parts: a clock process that sets `t <= not t;`, and a line after `end process;` that connects `q` to `t`.
---- hint2
Inside the clock edge: `t <= not t;`. After `end process;`: `q <= t;`
---- solution
```
process (clk)
begin
    if rising_edge(clk) then
        t <= not t;
    end if;
end process;
q <= t;
```
---- done
`q` flips half as often as the clock ticks: a **divide by two**. It is the first step of every counter.
